From 6f9b889dae6937ce16b08f902ad60865accc3f4d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: port <port@localhost>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 08:46:21 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 06/14] ruffle_sdl: Ruffle on SDL2 and GLES 3 for handhelds

A small front end (new crate sdl/, binary ruffle_sdl) for Linux handhelds
that have SDL2 and a GLES driver but no X11 or Wayland session. It replaces
Westonpack plus desktop Ruffle for handheld ports.

* Video: SDL opens a fullscreen window with a GLES 3.0 context. wgpu runs on
  that context through wgpu-hal external GLES adapter (no wgpu backend of its
  own: a second EGL display and context cost about 80 MB with Mesa). Ruffle
  draws each frame into one texture, which is blitted (Y flipped, scaled to
  fit) to the window. Debug labels are discarded, because Mali blob drivers
  crash in glPushDebugGroup (gfx-rs/wgpu#8937). --gl-finish replaces fence
  waits by glFinish for drivers with a broken glClientWaitSync; --render-size
  draws at a smaller size and scales up.
* Input: SDL keyboard, text and mouse events (on handhelds these come from
  gptokeyb virtual devices).
* Audio: Ruffle mixer through SDL audio (the output the firmware is set up
  for); cpal/ALSA as the fallback, then no audio.
* Loading: files only, through core NullNavigatorBackend relative to the SWF
  folder, with its executor polled in the main loop (so the Classic Pack shell
  can load its worlds, with no reqwest, tokio or OpenSSL in the binary).
* fscommand: "quit" ends the loop; "relaunch <args>" prints
  "RUFFLE_RELAUNCH <args>" and quits, so the port launch script can start Ruffle
  again straight into the next world (one world in memory at a time).
* Stage: --stage-scale fit (default) centres and scales the stage to fit
  whatever the movie asks for; --stage-scale movie keeps the movie own scale
  mode and alignment without letterbox (the Classic Pack shell lays itself out
  for 3:2 and 4:3 screens).
* Memory, for 1 GB devices: 1% extra collector work per tick
  (Player::add_gc_work, RUFFLE_GC_STEP), malloc_trim every 2 s
  (RUFFLE_TRIM_SECS), and one full collection plus a trim after a tick longer
  than 1 s, which is a load (RUFFLE_GC_LONG_TICK). glibc otherwise keeps the
  memory a load freed.
* RUFFLE_SDL_TIMING=1 logs the share of time spent in tick, render and swap
  every 2 s; long ticks are logged at info level.

request_device in ruffle_render_wgpu becomes public, so a front end with its
own adapter can ask for a device with Ruffle limits and features.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
